{"connection_id": "BC-937677695f8451ecc37e", "ref": "Augustine, City of God 20.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "opened books as recalled deeds", "note": "Augustine discusses the books opened at the last judgment as a disclosure of what people have done, possibly through divine recall rather than physical ledgers; this changes how the commentary's visible account can be imagined."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e50be133b00dc36c8c0", "ref": "Didache 16.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "community perfected at the last time", "note": "The commentary makes mutual exhortation and patience part of readiness for the end; the Didache tells believers to gather and seek what benefits their souls because faith will not profit them unless they are found perfected at the last time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-efa5f4ced0de94de2789", "ref": "Justin Martyr, First Apology 12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "everlasting outcome according to actions", "note": "The commentary's accounting divides people by what they do; Justin says people go to everlasting punishment or salvation according to the merit of their actions, an early Christian defense of judgment by conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2e900a6e7f37a33b6ae", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.13.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "impressive charity without love gains nothing", "note": "Even giving away all possessions gains nothing without love; this illuminates the commentary's apparently good efforts that prove valueless when judged by their real character."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9927fcfa219774c4ae34", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.58", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "labor not in vain", "note": "The faithful are urged to abound in the Lord's work because their labor is not vain; this directly reverses the surah's general human loss through faithful action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db5ad2edec5f364a11ce", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day reveals work; fire tests it", "note": "The commentary's deeds are brought into view and judged; Paul says the Day discloses each person's work and fire tests what sort it is, giving the image of examination a material test."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47b3aea0a9e6a17ca851", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "each work disclosed by fire", "note": "The Day exposes each person's work and fire tests its quality; this is a concrete counterpart to deeds becoming present and differentiated at judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15fc389277521bf1b838", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "work lost; person saved through fire", "note": "The commentary's gloss turns patience toward deeds of fire and punishment; Paul distinguishes the builder from work burned in the fire, saying the person may suffer loss yet be saved through fire. The image assigns loss to failed work rather than necessarily to the worker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ddfdbf3ef2bba7d52a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "work lost but worker saved", "note": "Paul says a burned work brings loss while its worker may still be saved through fire; this complicates the commentary's alignment of deficient works, personal loss, and fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9a6287037ddaace6b815", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hidden motives disclosed", "note": "The commentary says work is found present at judgment; Paul says the Lord will bring hidden things to light and disclose the intentions of hearts, adding inward motive to the public accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74d68db19cc9bf6e0fd5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hidden matters disclosed and praise allotted", "note": "At the Lord's coming, hidden things and heart motives are brought to light before praise is assigned; this parallels the commentary's deeds made present, while adding intent to visible work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7fb2b75fd4ccd154510", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "impartial judge; each person's work", "note": "The commentary says every human faces an account by deeds; Peter calls God the impartial judge of each person's work and makes that expectation shape conduct during exile."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8efcae09ba33e2c39f7c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "impartial judgment of each one's work", "note": "Peter says the Father judges impartially according to each person's work and tells believers to conduct themselves in reverent fear during their time away; this joins personal deeds, just measurement, and a limited present term."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6682829d45664ea657b5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.20", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endure suffering for doing good", "note": "Peter distinguishes endurance under punishment for sin from endurance when one does good and suffers; this clarifies the commentary's reversal of patience into work meriting fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55db70be1bf165893585", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.4.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "account to the judge of living and dead", "note": "The commentary says every person meets the account; Peter says wrongdoers will give account to the one ready to judge the living and the dead, explicitly universalizing the reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6bacd97baf01e3a0f56b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work of faith; labor of love; steadfast hope", "note": "The commentary's exception gathers faith, work, and patience in community; Paul remembers the Thessalonians' work produced by faith, labor prompted by love, and endurance grounded in hope."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e0c26ad7274264d79dd", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work of faith and patient hope", "note": "Paul remembers the community's work of faith and endurance of hope; this places action, faith, and patience together as the surah's exception does."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78ae0dab8d0f8491721f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "build one another up", "note": "The commentary's mutual exhortation makes the community responsible for one another; Paul tells the Thessalonians to encourage and build one another up in the immediate context of the Day of the Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b88b6caeaa3ed54ca41", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "patience with all", "note": "The commentary pairs mutual counsel with patience; Paul tells the community to admonish the idle, encourage the fainthearted, help the weak, and be patient with everyone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8fc82b63127f6f714dd9", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "each receives for bodily deeds", "note": "The commentary makes every person's work present at judgment; Paul says all must appear before Christ's judgment seat so each receives for what was done in the body, whether good or bad."}

